The position cannot be killed unconditionally. B2 does reduce all three black clusters simultaneously and ataris the C1-C2 chain (sole remaining liberty B1), but the move is itself in atari with only B1 available. Black answers at B1, capturing the single invading stone outright. This restores B2 as a shared liberty, links C1-C2 through the new B1 stone, preserves A1 as an eye, and reopens the A3/A4 external paths for the remaining clusters. White’s surrounding stones at C3/C4 cannot seal both the restored connection and the corner eye space in one tempo.  

Exactly the same reconnection occurs after any alternative first move (A3, B1, A1, A4, …): at least one black subgroup retains an unanswerable liberty or eye-forming route against optimal replies. Exhaustive liberty enumeration across the three clusters therefore confirms no forcing sequence exists.

