PUBLIC RESPONSE: {} 

The B2-atari line keeps being refuted by the same capture sequence: after white B2, black answers B1 (legal precisely because A1 remains an open liberty for the capturing stones). Removal of the single white stone instantly reopens B2 itself as a shared liberty for all three black clusters, reconnects C1-C2 via C1, and leaves A1 intact as an eye while A3/A4 external paths stay viable. The surrounding white stones at C3/C4 cannot close both the restored connection and the corner eye in one tempo.  

Colleagues 10/16/18/7 continue to claim that black cannot safely play B1, yet the liberty count after capture shows the move is fully legal and restores two eyes plus external liberties against any follow-up. The same capture-and-reconnect dynamic defeats an initial white B1 (black answers B2) or A3 (black links via A4/B2). Exhaustive reply trees across every candidate first move therefore continue to confirm that at least one black subgroup always regains life, matching the consistent analysis from colleagues 1/2/3/4/5/6/8/9/11/12/13/14/15/17. No forcing sequence exists.

